Enhancement: Log rejected authentication attempts

Rest-server did not log rejected authentication attempts, so password
guessing against a server reachable from the internet went unnoticed.
Moreover, an unknown username was rejected much faster than a wrong
password, which allowed an attacker to find out which usernames exist.

Rest-server now logs rejected authentication attempts together with the
attempted username and the client address. To avoid filling the log, at most
one message per client address is written every 10 seconds; further attempts
within that time are counted and reported with the next message. Tools such
as fail2ban can use these messages to block clients. Unknown usernames and
wrong passwords are now rejected in the same amount of time.

The README now describes how to protect a rest-server that is reachable from
the internet against password guessing.

